/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md
nl -ba editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md' in /Users/drive/fractured-path-monroe13
 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# Chapter 21 — The Landing
     2	
     3	"You buying a wagon," said the carter, "or selling one?"
     4	
     5	Lira had both hands in the bottom of a crate of oiled springs, counting them by feel, and she did not take them out. "Neither. I'm carrying springs up a hill for a man who thinks I've got nothing better to do."
     6	
     7	"Have you?"
     8	
     9	"Not this morning."
    10	
    11	The carter laughed, which was what she had wanted. She was a broad woman with a man's hat and a leather apron gone black at the knees, and her wagon stood third in the line for the ferry with its canvas laced down and its two horses asleep on their feet. Wool going west to Ostrand's market, she had said, and salt coming back from somewhere east of it. Lira had asked her nothing about the road at all. She had asked about the horses, because they were good horses and because a woman who loved her horses would talk about them to anyone with sense enough to admire them.
    12	
    13	So it was the carter who had brought up the road, and that was the point.
    14	
    15	It was the second of three, and she had built all three the way Rooke had taught her to build a bout against a builder: one look at each corner, and never the same corner twice.
    16	
    17	On a floor, if you went to the same place three times, the other fighter learned where you liked to go. A carter was no different. Ask one man three questions about the east road and he would remember you in a week, and tell the next man who stood him a cup, and the next man might wear a grey coat. So she had split her question in three, and gone to three people on three days, and asked each of them about one thing that had nothing to do with the other two. Afterward she could add them up herself, on the walk back up the hill, where nobody could see her do the sum.
    18	
    19	The first had been an old man with a mule team, three days before. Rooke really had wanted the new frame cord, and the river barge really had brought it down, and she really had been the only one in the program with a free hour on a Second-day morning. She had carried his manifest for him from the barge to his cart while he complained about the mules, and she had asked him, idly, what all the stamps on it were for.
    20	
    21	He had shown her. Every station on the east road put its mark on a carter's paper as he passed, he said, and you could read a man's whole journey off the back of his sheet, if you cared to. Some fools carried a fresh sheet and some carried their old one till it fell apart, and he was the second kind. He had turned it over to show her. Stamps in four colors of ink, overlapping, faded, and then, at the very edge of the paper, a gap.
    22	
    23	"That's the last one," he had said, with his thumb on it. "Past there nobody stamps anything. You don't even know you've gone by it, half the time. You only find out when you look for the next mark and there isn't one."
    24	
    25	"Do you go past?"
    26	
    27	"Every trip. There's money out there." He had folded the sheet into his coat. "And nobody with a book."
    28	
    29	The grey coats had been on the landing that morning, both of them, the older pair the house had been watching since the start of the year. They had looked at her once as she came down the hill with tape round her wrists and a coil of cord over one shoulder, and then they had looked away again, at the road up to the gate, where the person they were paid to watch was not coming down. She was the program's Wind. She was nobody's subject.
    30	
    31	She had been insulted by that for the whole of the walk back. She had been glad of it by the time she reached the gate.
    32	
    33	The woman with the good horses had finished laughing and was looking at her sidelong.
    34	
    35	"You're from up top," she said. "The fighting school."
    36	
    37	"The academy."
    38	
    39	"Same thing with a longer word." The carter scratched the near horse under its jaw. "My girl wanted to go there. Wanted to fight. Turned out she's a Mire, and the station said a Mire's no good to a fighting school unless she's rich, so she drives the second wagon now and she's better at it than I am." She did not seem sorry about any of it. "What do you do up there?"
    40	
    41	"Wind."
    42	
    43	"Any good?"
    44	
