editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:219:"No." Vastin did not soften it. "That is the other half of why I asked for you. What you do now makes no difference to it. A year ago it would have. A thing you do is an answer, and answers are for questions, and they have stopped asking. Nothing answers a conclusion."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-220-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-221----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-222-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-223-The clocks went round. Cael let them, for a while. Then he set down the question he had brought up the stair with him, because he did not think the hour would have a better place for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-224-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-225-"How did you evaluate me?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-226-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:227:Vastin did not ask what he meant. He looked, for a moment, very slightly pleased.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-228-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-229-"With the wrong instruments, for three days," he said. "I came up to the bluff with a file and a plate and a set of questions, and all three told me nothing. A man in my trade has one great temptation, and I gave in to it. I thought the nothing was something being hidden from me." He moved the left hand. "It is the oldest mistake there is. When an instrument won't read, you'd sooner believe it's broken than believe you've pointed it at a thing it was never made to measure."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-230-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-231-"And then?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-232-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-233-"Then you answered my fourth question. In full, truthfully, every word of it. And I learned nothing whatever from the answer." Something that was nearly amusement moved in his face. "I have done that to other men for forty years. Nobody had ever done it to me. From that question on I stopped measuring what you could do and started watching how you went about it." He looked at Cael steadily. "The method is not a boy's. That is my whole opinion of you. It's in no file. There is no line on any form where it would go."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-234-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-235-He glanced down at his own left hand on the table, and then up again.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-263-He went down the stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-264-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-265-Brom was sitting on the step under the arch with his forearms on his knees, looking at the copy line. He got up when Cael came out into the light, slowly, the bad shoulder first, and stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-266-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-267-"An hour," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-268-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-269-Brom tipped his head back and looked up at the face of the tower, at the great clock every other clock in the Concourse was set by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-270-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:271:"Fifty-one minutes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-272-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-273-They went back across the court together. The copy line had moved perhaps twenty yards. Brom did not ask anything, all the way across, and Cael did not offer anything. At the guesting-house door Brom stopped with his hand on the latch and looked at him once, the way he looked at a floor before he stood on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-274-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-275-"Good," he said, and opened the door. That was the last either of them ever said about the step.
--
