editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-139-She did not read it out. She turned the instrument round on the table so that it faced the rest of them, put her finger on the paragraph, and took the finger away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-140-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-141-The counsel laid her rule under it and read it aloud.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-142-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-143-*7. The petitioner notes, and adopts as its own, the advisory entered in the prior year by an evaluator of the Compact upon the fielding clause of the tournament charter, which reads in full: "The clause says what it says. The enrollment is lawful on its face and adjudicated at public record. If the Compact wishes practitioners of this kind not to compete, it should write different rules — not misread the ones it has."*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-144-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-145-Nobody spoke.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-146-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:147:"*The clause says what it says,*" said Karis at last. "I said that. On the bench under the thorn at the first bend, the morning the entry went down the hill. *It says what it says all the same.* I said it to you, Cael." She laid her palms on the wood, one either side of the paper. "And somebody said it to them, in writing, a year ago. The Archmarshal. Vastin. You told us at Norhold there'd been three sentences of advisory, and none of us ever saw them. There they are."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-148-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-149-"And they've taken his advice," said the counsel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-150-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-151-"They've taken his advice." Karis's face changed then.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-152-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-153-Cael had watched that face for three years. He had seen it angry and amused and bored and cold, and once or twice, very late, tired enough to be kind, and at Norhold in the moment the stoppage was called. He had never seen it like this. She was looking at a paragraph on cream paper as if the paper had moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-154-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-155-"He told them how," she said. "Write different rules, he said, don't misread the old ones. So here are the new ones, written, and when the seat asks them by what right, they'll point at him. The honest man's own sentence, for a warrant." She took her hands off the table. "Write it down. This is where I was frightened. The seventh paragraph. Not the fourteenth chapter, not whatever's coming. This."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-156-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-157-"Written," said the counsel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-158-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-159-She had a page at her elbow now that had not been there when the night began, headed in a small exact hand: *Halcenvane: the Compact's petition. Working minute. Privileged.* She wrote Karis's sentence on it word for word, with the time by the hall's clock, and blotted it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-160-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-161-Cael put it in the observation notebook as well, since word for word was the only way that notebook took anything. Then he wrote the other thing under it. It had come while Karis was speaking, plainly, like a sum totting itself up at the foot of a column.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-162-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:163:*Vastin, in the room full of clocks: the people who want this to stop are not going to use official channels much longer. I have never held a more official thing than this paper. Nineteen leaves, a seal, an escort at a walk, a form of joint service I had never seen. So either he was wrong about the channels, or this is the last thing they'll send through one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-164-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-165-*And the warrant on it is his. He warned me in a room where nothing was written down. They answered in a room where everything is, and signed it with his name.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-166-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-167-He looked at the two lines for a while.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-168-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-169-He found he was not frightened. Not yet. He was something nearer cold. It was the feeling he had had on the morning of his Kindling at Denvash, standing in a frame that had nothing to say about him and was perfectly calm about it. The fright would come later, he knew, at the window, in the dark, where there was time for it; not here, where there was work.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-170-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-171-He looked up, and Karis was looking at him across the lamps.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-141-He read it twice and found nothing. He had not expected to find anything; Karis did not make tables with things in them to find. He laid it on the sill and began to undress, and then stopped with one boot off and looked at it again.
