editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-143-Why first? Why tonight?
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-144-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-145-He sat down on the bed with the boot in his hand and thought about the inspection year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-146-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-147-He remembered the room by its east wall, because that was where the strangers had been. Four chairs set out by Bracken at a guest's distance from one another. The delegation's counsel in the nearest, untying a folder's ribbon with her teeth. Ilsev beside her, sharpening a pencil into a fold of paper on her knee. Havel with a board across his knees, writing the date. And in the fourth chair the Archmarshal, with nothing in his hands at all. Three hours of instruments on the oak under those four chairs: the frames, the board, the pendulum ticking in its case. Then Gault reading his note aloud at the trestle, and the Ash instructor signing, and the Mire instructor adding her four lines about the faulty frame, and the clerk bringing the stamp down on the house's copy. *Renewed.* That had been the day, and the house's copy had been stamped on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-148-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:149:But the house's copy was not the only one. Bracken had told him so at the counter two days afterward, when the line went into the enrollment book. The delegation's counsel had asked, in writing, that the registry's own copy of the note go up the road under the delegation's countersignature before the registry sealed it into his file, and Bracken had objected on both sides of a sheet, and lost. So that copy had waited. And when it came back down, sealed and dated, so that the enrollee of record could initial its receipt before it went up again for good, Gault had called him into his office to do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-150-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-151-He put the boot down and went to the drawer and took out the old volume of the Log.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-152-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-153-It was full; there was no room in it for one word more. But every word that was in it was still there, and he knew roughly where in its thick back half the inspection year began. He turned the pages under the lamp, past fights and figures and the names of rooms, to the long entry for the evaluation day, the one that ended on *wondering*. Then he turned forward a page at a time, counting the days between entries by the bells and the weekday names, until he found two short lines he had nearly forgotten writing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-154-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-155-*Gault's office. Initialled the registry's copy of the renewal, back from the delegation under seal, dated today. Up the road again in the noon bag.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-156-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-157-Nine days.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-171-"Show me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-172-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-173-He opened the old Log on the table beside her elbow at the evaluation day, and laid his finger beside its last word without touching it, and then turned forward to the two short lines and did the same. She read both, the way she read anything with a date in it, once for the words and once for the numbers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-174-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-175-"Under the delegation's countersignature," she said. "Their counsel asked for that?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-176-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-177-"In writing. Bracken objected in writing. She won."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-178-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:179:Karis said nothing to that. She laid the renewals table down square, took the whole leaf out of the answer, and drew a clean sheet toward her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-180-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-181-He watched her rule the grid again from nothing, column by column, and thought that a footnote would have cost her a minute. A small number in the cell, a line at the foot, and the nine days were explained. She was going to spend an hour instead. She had told the counsel the day before that she had no hours to spare that week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-182-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-183-"You're thinking a footnote," said Karis, without looking up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-184-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-185-"I was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-186-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:187:She went on ruling. Every row this time gave two days, the trestle and the seal, and the first renewal's row gave the reason for the gap in a single line. Under the grid she wrote where each day could be found: the house's enrollment book for the trestle; the registry's own file, to be produced on request, for the seals; and for the gap, *the enrollee's contemporaneous record, available to the seat*. Then she reached across the table for the instrument, opened it at schedule two, and laid it beside the old table she had just taken out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-188-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-189-"Read them together," she said. "The way he will."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-190-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-191-He read them together. Schedule two listed his file's standing entries by the registry's own dates, and the registry dated a renewal by its seal. Her old table dated it by the trestle. Two papers on one desk, about one afternoon, nine days apart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-192-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-193-"Now you're the seat," said Karis. "One of these two was drawn up by people who are careful. Which?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-194-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-195-He did not answer. He did not have to.
--
