editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-237-"The scales?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-238-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-239-"Bracken weighs things," said Karis. "He's going to want the number." She ate another mouthful, slowly, as if she had just remembered that food was a thing a person did. "So am I."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-240-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:241:Across the table, Seln cut his bread into exact squares, and buttered one, and did not look up. But when Karis said *bones*, his knife had stopped for a moment above the board, and Cael, who had been watching for it, had counted the moment, and it was a long one.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md-179-One of the pair at the road's foot was grey in the beard and leaned on a stick he did not seem to need. The other kept his hands in his coat and looked at the river. Neither had raised his eyes to the bluff since the change. The younger coats of other years had craned at the walls like visitors to a cathedral, and shifted their feet a little more each week as the place came clear to them. These two stood as if there were nothing on the hill they had not already been told about. They had the patience of men near the end of a long errand, who know the one thing they have been sent to see and do not mean to miss it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md-180-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md-181-He watched until the light went, and they did not look up once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md-182-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md-183-He wrote it on the wall, with the notebook flat on the coping and his back to the river.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md-184-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md-185-*Third evening. The change on the bell, both pairs, as every year. Different men. These have no questions about the hill; whoever sent them answered all of those first. Men with no questions get sent at the end of something, not the start. You don't post your best men to learn a gate. You post them to be standing there when it opens.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md-186-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:187:*I don't know what they're waiting for. Seln says senior men. Bracken watches the road. Vastin said a sitting.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md-188-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md-189-*Three people who never guess, and none of them has guessed out loud. I'm not going to be the first.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md-190-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md-191-He shut the book. Across the river the ferryman lit the lamp on the landing's post, and the two coats there stood in its light without moving, as patient as the post.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md-23-*Seln: at the copying table every working day of the recess, and at the middle of the bench every evening. Nobody has said a word about the bench. Nobody needs to.*
