editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md-75-Four.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md-76-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md-77-The spark was Karis's, and he used it only by her leave. She had given him that leave a long time ago, standing, and never taken it back. There was a copper basin on the windowsill that the porters filled for the floor in the hot weeks. He emptied it out of the window into the gutter and set it on the bench, and laid two fingers against its rim. The spark went out of him the way a word goes out of a mouth. The copper ticked. A smell came up from it like a struck flint, and the rim was warm under his fingers. Two contacts a bout was the rule in public. It was a house rule, not the registry's, and at Norhold he had broken it once, on purpose. He set the basin down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md-78-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md-79-Five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md-80-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md-81-He stood in the middle of the floor and did not hurry the sixth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md-82-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:83:The quiet thing from the stair he did not try. He never tried it; it was not a thing that needed calling. It came by itself in the gaps of the day whether he called it or not, and he had paid rent on it every day since he took it, from the moment he woke to the moment he slept, spent or unspent. It was sealed. It would stay sealed until the debt it was sealed for was spoken, and nobody had spoken it yet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md-84-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md-85-That left the last. Daeva's storm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md-86-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md-87-He had carried it eleven weeks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md-88-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md-89-It had come to him in the fourth exchange of the hardest bout of his life, in front of eight thousand people, out of the hands of a Gold who had begun the fight herself, and who had looked at him afterward on the stone as if he had asked her a question nobody had ever asked her before. The notice that came after it was the first that had ever said *Gold*. It was also the first that had ever warned him that what it gave him might not hold. *Stability: provisional — architecture under load.* The notice had found him on one knee on the stone, with a healer's fingers in his hand and eight thousand people looking down, and he had read it through twice there, before anybody strapped him. He had felt its words every day since.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md-90-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md-91-Gold was heavy. Everything else he carried had been shifting to make room for it, slowly, the way the furniture in a house shifts when somebody carries a great iron chest in through the front door. For weeks after Norhold he had reached for the Wind and got something else. Some mornings it was the push, and his whole body shoved at nothing. Once, on the road, it was the read, and he had stood in a waystation yard knowing the exact weight of the water butt and going nowhere. The healers had said a month. His own count had said more, and his own count had been right.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md-119-Cael had his fingers on the copy-slip. He had turned half away from the counter when Seln spoke again, in exactly the same voice, as if reading the next line of the same form.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md-120-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md-121-"The second volume's own index runs a dozen years behind the volume. The digests are better for the late years." He had already turned back to the copying table. "If anyone should happen to be working from the second volume."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md-122-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md-123-Cael went out through the wing doors and along the covered walk, and did not let himself smile until he was under the north arch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md-124-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md-125-He had thought, for two years, that Seln did not give things. He saw now that this was wrong. Seln gave things all the time. He gave forms before anybody had asked for them, and a sheaf under a door before dawn, and a correction about an index to a boy at a counter who could carry it upstairs. He gave them in a way that made thanks impossible, because there was no record of the thing and so nothing to thank him for. It was not modesty. Cael did not think it was even kindness, exactly. It was a man who had spent his life on the side of a counter where everything that passed across it was written down, making very sure that the one thing he chose to give stayed off the paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md-126-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:127:He thought about the quiet thing he carried, sealed, and the debt it was sealed for, which the man at the copying table did not know he was owed. He thought that it would be a very hard debt to pay to somebody who would not let anything be paid to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md-128-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md-129-He went up the library stair and told Karis about the index. She went straight to the digests without a word. Twenty minutes later she found that one of her ninety-one, a coastal panel that had sat a member short, carried a year she had taken from the second volume's index, and the index had it a dozen years late. She ruled the year through and wrote the right one above it. It changed nothing in the argument. It went in anyway.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md-130-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md-131-Bracken read the whole answer in two nights.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md-132-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md-133-He read it at his counter, every leaf, from the address to the last schedule. The oil book took a third line and a fourth. He did not skim. Cael came past the records-hall windows late on the first night on his way back from the north wall, and saw him through the glass, a narrow grey shape bent over a single lamp, turning a leaf, reading it, initialling its corner, turning the next. A registrar's initials on a leaf were a promise about the leaf. Bracken did not make promises he had not read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md-134-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md-135-On the second night it rained.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md-31-On his feet he was a different kind of man from the two outside. He had soft hands and good boots that had not been walked in, and a clerk's grey coat kept very clean, with a small seal of office in dull silver at the collar that Cael did not know. He was perhaps fifty, with thin hair combed close and the careful face of somebody who has set a great many things down on a great many counters and has never once been set down himself. He set the case on the counter and laid his hand on it and waited.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md-32-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md-33-Withrow came in from the inner door thirty seconds later.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md-34-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md-35-She came down the long hall as if she had been sent for about a gutter. Cael had watched her cross courts and corridors for two years and knew the walk as well as he knew Brom's step on a stair, even and unhurried, the walk of a woman who had decided long ago that nothing would ever be allowed to set its pace. With half the hill already talking outside, he understood that she had chosen it on the stair and was holding it now the way a carter holds a nervous horse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md-36-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md-37-She stopped at the counter beside Bracken, and the officer bowed to her, to a depth that somebody had once measured, and took a card from his coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md-38-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:39:"An instrument of the registry, under seal," he read from the card, in a voice with nothing in it. "For joint service upon the Chancellor of Halcenvane Academy and upon the enrollee of record whom it names, the two together and in their own persons. Service to be acknowledged in both their hands. The seal to stay whole until it is." He lowered the card. "Are both parties present?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md-40-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md-41-"Both parties are present," said Withrow.
