editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md-157-He had known the boy would be at it. The house had said it would attend every sitting, and the house kept what it said. Havel did not look at him, because the rotation manual's page on courtesy said that an officer of a seat would show the parties ordinary courtesy and no more, and because looking at the boy was not ordinary. He had looked before. On a pump step at Ardenmere, where looking had been the work. Twice at Greyvane, from the recorder's desk below the bench. Three times on the oak at the bluff, from a chair on the east wall: once as the boy came in, and twice when the frame broke, the second quicker than the first. He remembered every one. He did not mean to add to them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md-158-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md-159-So he kept the times instead.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md-160-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md-161-*The seat opened at the second bell.* The clerk read the designation; Havel wrote the minute it ended. *The petitioner's procurator entered the petition.* Jent stood at his table with his leaf in one hand, read the petition's working paragraphs in an even voice that gave no one of them more weight than another, named his nine exhibits and sat; Havel wrote the minute he stood and the minute he sat. Nine minutes. *The respondent's counsel entered the answer.* She rose, small and grey with a steel rule in her hand, and summarized eleven pounds in a voice that did not hurry and did not linger; twenty-three minutes. Havel took the receipts for both lists of exhibits in the receipt book, nine items and a hundred and seventy-three, and wrote the numbers, and initialled them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md-162-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md-163-Then the senior chair passed the minute up to the platform for the seat's initial, and it came back down, and Havel saw the seal.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md-164-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:165:The seat's seal was pressed into the foot of the minute's first leaf, in the grey wax the registry used for proceedings. He gave it the attention he gave every seal that crossed a table he sat at, which was close attention, because looking at seals was part of the work. It was the seat's own. The registry at Ostrand, the old device with the river and the scale. He had seen it on ten thousand minutes. There was nothing in it, or about it, that he had not seen before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md-166-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md-167-He noted that there was nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md-168-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md-169-He thought, sitting at the junior chair with the minute in front of him, about the five sheets.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md-170-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md-171-They were not with him. They never came to a sitting. They were in his kit at the inn, between the second manual and the third, where they had lived since the night in Norhold when he had written the fifth on a narrow table behind the east tower, with the line he had read upside down on the senior seat's desk at its head. Five entries. The first was a pump step at Ardenmere, and a form with four questions on it, and a mark in the administrative layer of the boy's file that had no office behind it. The last was a sentence closing a door that the first had found open. In between were three more, and every one of the five recorded something that had happened, and nothing that he made of it. That was the rule of the sheets.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md-172-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md-173-He had been told, once, a long time ago, in a room with the door shut, that the mark was not his clearance and he was to let it be. He had let it be. He had only written down that it was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md-174-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:175:He looked at the ordinary seal on the ordinary minute and felt, very faintly, the shape of a sixth sheet, the way you feel the shape of a word you cannot quite remember. And then he looked for the fact to put on it, and there was no fact. A seat sitting by its seal was lawful. A hood was lawful. A procurator who conceded everything was lawful and, in Havel's experience, sensible. The seal was the seal it should have been. Nothing in this room had done anything that was not in the code.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md-176-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md-177-A sixth sheet would need something that had happened. Nothing had happened. A form had been filled in by people in a room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md-178-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md-179-He went back to the times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md-180-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md-181-*The seat confirmed the schedule. The evaluation seat to be empanelled at a date to be notified. The seat rose.* He wrote the minute it rose, and blotted the leaf, and squared the receipt book on top of the door log, and did not look up at the respondent's table even when the scrape of its chairs went down the long room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md-182-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md-183-That evening, at the inn, he took the five sheets out from between the manuals and read them through, every line, in order, the first to the fifth. He read them twice. Then he put them back between the manuals, the same way round, and buckled the kit, and sat at the window over the bridge for a while with his hands folded and the right one aching.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md-79-He did not look up when Cael came in. He held out his left hand, without turning his head, with a small bundle of letters in it.
