editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md-85-The porter had explained, from his stool, before Cael had asked, that he had done his shoulder in lifting a crate on the Fourth-day. The assistant to the assessment wing had come down on the Fifth-day and offered, and had done the sort ever since, and did it very well, though not, the porter said, the way he would have done it himself. Seln did not answer this, or seem to hear it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md-86-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:87:He sorted the district bag into three piles on the bench. Cael had never watched anybody sort post before with any attention, and he found that it was not one act but four, done so quickly they looked like one. Seln took a letter out of the bag. He read the address. He turned it over and read the seal. He laid it on its pile. He did not hold any letter for longer than another; he did not let his eyes rest on any seal more than on any other. A man watching from the end of the bench would have said he was sorting the post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md-88-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md-89-Cael watched the piles instead of the man.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md-90-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md-91-The first pile was the house's, for the halls and the residence, and it went into his hand as it grew. The second was the assessment wing's: anything addressed to the wing, its counter, its instructors, its office. The third was the registrar's, for Bracken's counter and the records hall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md-92-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:93:The third pile was the tallest. That was not strange in itself; Bracken's counter got more post than anywhere on the hill. What Cael noticed, after a while, was the seals on it. Half of the letters in Bracken's pile carried the grey wax of the district seat at Ostrand. In the wing's pile there was not one. Not one grey seal, in a bag that held forty letters from the district.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md-94-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md-95-He thought back. The wing had always had district post. The provision's hours were signed in the wing, and the district seat had written to the wing about them, about observers and schedules and the figures in the wing's book, every few weeks for two years. He had stood at the wing's counter himself and watched the desk clerk open grey seals.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md-96-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md-97-He did not say anything. Seln did not say anything. Seln laid another grey seal on Bracken's pile, at exactly the pace he had laid the last one.
