editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md-25-He did not read the order until the hall had already cooled. By then it was on Bracken's board in two lines of the registrar's small square hand: *Received at a quarter past two, at the rail. Read entire, acknowledged, duplicate obtained and collated.* Under it Bracken had underlined *at the rail* twice. Karis had the duplicate, and passed it along the bench to Cael without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md-26-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md-27-*The respondent institution is directed to produce at the fourth sitting of the seat the complete records of evaluation-linked floor work of the enrollee of record, Caelen Hesk-ward, for the academic year to date, in the form in which they are kept.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md-28-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md-29-One sentence. Wing three's figures, every bout of the year in the clerk's careful hand, twenty-three and a third, twenty-two and two-thirds, the band breathing inside its range as it had been built to. Cael looked from the order to the two strokes under *at the rail* on Bracken's board, and back.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md-185-They were two days going home, and it rained on the second, a long fine rain that came in at the coach's window frames and ran down the inside of the glass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md-186-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md-187-Cael wrote up the meet in the coach, on his knee, while the rain ran down the window and Brom slept and Lira, opposite, slept too, with her head against the window frame and one hand inside her coat. Karis had the fourth sitting's papers open and the production order's duplicate pinned to the top of them with a clip. Every so often she took the plain card out of the wallet's back and looked at it, and put it away again. Seln, in his corner, was awake for once, with his eyes on the wet fields going by. Cael did not ask him what he had seen from the loft that he had not said at supper. He had learned two years ago that the only way to get anything from Seln was to leave a space beside him and wait to see if anything came and sat in it. Lira, asleep against the window frame, kept her hand inside her coat the whole of the second day, flat over something folded, and did not take it out even when the coach went over the ford and woke everybody else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md-188-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md-189-Nothing did. At the ferry the two grey coats were on the landing in their usual places, under the rain, as if it were not raining. One of them watched the program wagon go aboard behind the coach, with the colors trunk lashed on top of it, and did not look at the coach at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md-190-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md-191-*Lira, the confluence, the Silver Rank Eight: two touches to one, on the fourth. Twenty-seven to twenty-eight. Six bursts, one of them borrowed. She asked me to watch her feet and tell her first, and I did, and I'll keep doing it as long as she asks. The floor had four facts on it and I found three. Rooke wrote the fourth as his. It was mine as much as his. I looked at that corner too.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md-192-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md:193:*The order: one sentence, served at the hour it would be seen. Fifty-one seconds there and fifty-one back. A woman with a baby on her shoulder asked who thanks a man for that. The hall went on liking us and stopped saying so, which is worse, because there's nothing to answer.*
