editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md-239-Karis finished her copying and set down her pen. Nobody touched Lira's book. Brom sat with his elbows on his knees. And Seln went on looking at the order, longer than any of them, with his hands flat and still on either side of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md-240-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:241:"Fifty-one minutes," said Brom, to the floor between his boots. "That clock room at Norhold. And you came out of it with one sentence for us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md-242-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:243:He did not say which sentence. He did not need to. Every one of them had carried it home from Norhold and up this hill, and Cael heard it again now in the Archmarshal's dry voice, among forty clocks: *Because the people who want this to stop are not going to use official channels much longer.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md-244-
