editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md-247-"Nor have I, and every docket that came up this hill in thirteen years has crossed my counter." He took the sheet back. "A docket gives the day and the room and the panel. Nobody lets it guess at the end. Some district clerk got tired of guessing quietly and wrote his guess where we could all see it." He looked at the word over his spectacles as if it might go away. "I'd like to shake his hand. I'd also like to have him moved. I'll be writing to the district." He held the sheet out across the table to Brom. "Well. They've as good as painted it on the door."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md-248-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md-249-Brom wiped his hands on his napkin and took the sheet, and read it, and folded it twice along the creases the district clerk had put in it. Then he unbuttoned his coat and put it in the left inside pocket, with the first docket and the summons and the Velmere letters, and buttoned the coat again, and pressed the buttons down one after another with his palm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md-250-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md-251-"Thank you, Registrar."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md-252-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md-253-Bracken's eyes rested on the buttons. He said nothing about where the paper had gone, and went back up the refectory between the long tables.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md-254-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:255:The second envelope came up with the evening post. Cael saw it on the post-room shelf when he went for his own letters, a square cream envelope with a dark red seal pressed with the bar and the ring, and he knew the seal. Brom took it from the shelf at the eighth bell. He did not open it in the post room or the court. He took it upstairs and shut his door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md-256-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md-257-Late that night, crossing the court from the north wall, Cael looked up at the residence front, because he always did. Brom's window was lit, and lit wrong. The lamp that had stood on the sill side of the table for two years was over on the far wall now, on the washstand, so that the light fell everywhere in the room except on the table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md-258-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md-259-Lira was sitting on the stair in the dark when he came in, with her bad leg out straight along the step.
