editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md-117-"If he asked, I'd sit with him while he did. I'd read it to him if he wanted, and never say one word about it after." She climbed two more steps. "He won't ask. He's carrying it the way he carries everything, so nobody else has to. And the worst of it is that's exactly what we'd all want him to do, if it were any of us." She stopped at her own landing. "So we don't ask. And he carries it. And the letter sits in his coat getting heavier." She went in at her door and shut it quietly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md-118-
