editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-98-There was a wrapper in the waste-basket, and he did not remember throwing it there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-99-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-100-It was the outer wrapper of a seat-courier's packet, heavy grey paper stamped across in red, *BY SEAT COURIER. BY HAND.* He looked at it for some time. Then he looked at the boy's folder lying shut on his blotter, and opened it, and found on top of his own nine leaves a single sheet of registry-seat stock with a seal pressed into it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-101-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-102-He did not remember putting it there either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-103-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-104-He went back for it, a step at a time, as he would have made a witness go back. A tread on the stair that had not been his clerk's tread: single steps, quick, the whole flight, no pause at the top. The clerk in the doorway with the packet held out in both hands. His own face, arranged. Then the two sentences. Then, it seemed, the folder, and the window, and a long while of the porter and the pigeons that had gone into him without leaving any trace at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-105-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:106:*The proceedings' evaluation seat is fully constituted. Supplementary findings are not contemplated by the schedule.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-107-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-108-He read it again, to be sure it said what he remembered. It did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-109-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-110-By noon there was a refusal in *done* that had taken him a quarter of an hour to write, where such things usually took him a quarter of a minute. A guild hall had asked for more time to answer a finding on its own assessors, and he had said no; but he had said it at length, giving the hall the section it had failed to meet, the officer it might apply to instead, and the date by which it should. He had signed it with his name entire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-111-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-112-Under it in the tray lay the first sheet he had started, with a single half-line on it, struck through corner to corner so that it could still be read. *The hall's request for further time is not contemplated by*
