editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-124-"Will you be getting home tonight, sir? It's set in to rain."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-125-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-126-The clerk had said it from the doorway at the sixth bell, with the day's wallet against his chest, looking not at the desk but past it at the window, where the evening had gone close and grey. It was the only thing he had ever said in that room in nine years that had nothing to do with paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-127-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:128:Vastin had looked up from the plain sheets he was writing on. "Later than usual," he had said. "But yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-129-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:130:The clerk had gone down without another word, and come back a few minutes later, unasked, with a candle already lit in a tin dish, and set it on the corner of the desk by the crooked press. The house put out the lamps on that floor at the half-bell after six. In the morning Vastin found the candle in the consumables ledger, entered in the clerk's tidy hand against the Archmarshal's name and not the floor's. He had not told him to put it there. He left it as it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-131-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-132-He opened the lowest drawer to see that the folder was in it. It was. It had a tab, and the tab had a heading in his own hand, the only one of its kind he had written in forty years, because every finding he had ever made had come with somewhere to be sent.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-133-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-134-*Pending a proceeding worth sending it to.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-135-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-136-He did not take the nine pages out. He had read them through once last night by the candle, with the rain starting on the glass, and then he had done a thing he did not often do: he had read the last paragraph aloud to the empty room, quietly, to hear whether it would stand being spoken.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-137-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-138-"Offered to the proceedings at Ostrand under the code's two provisions; declined without citation as outside their schedule. The schedule itself is not disputed. One thing only is set down. A schedule that leaves no room for the one personal assessment of the subject the Compact holds was drawn for a purpose the evaluator was never told and cannot name from his desk. Where the outcome of an evaluation is settled before the evaluator arrives, it is not the subject who is being measured."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-139-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-140-It had stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-141-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-142-The rest he could have said without the pages. That the proceedings had in their hands already the one honest instrument there was, three years of the record, kept by officers who could see. That a re-evaluation whose answer was known in advance was a procedure wearing an evaluation's coat. That when the standard protocol fell silent in front of this boy the silence was the protocol's own. That the protocol could not read him. Somewhere in the middle was a sentence he had spoken at inquiries for forty years about frames, *the instrument is not at fault; it has told you where its reach ends*, and last night, for the first time, he had written it about a person, and sat with his pen lifted for a long while before he let it stand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-143-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:144:He had gone home at the ninth bell, later than usual, as he had said he would. The porter had been at the lodge door with his lamp, looking out at the rain, and had said "Late, sir," in the voice of a man noting a thing for the record, and Vastin had agreed that it was. The street had been empty and loud with water. He had walked it without hurrying, with his collar up and his left hand in his coat, and he had found that he was not thinking about the seat, or the five chairs, or who had sat down in them. He was thinking about a boy standing still in a frame that had stuttered and gone dark, three days on the bluff, not moving, not asking what it meant, waiting to be told what the instruments had found. And he was thinking that, of all the officers who had stood in rooms with that boy, he was the one who had written down what that silence meant, and that nobody was going to be allowed to read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-145-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-146-It was not a theory. He did not hold with theories about subjects. It was only a thing he had noticed, walking, and he let it walk beside him to his door, and left it on the step.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-147-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md-148-Now he closed the drawer. At the head of the first page, under the subject and the basis and the date, on the line the sheet kept for the name of whoever a finding was meant for, he had written nothing at all.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md-1-# Chapter 26 — One Question
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md-2-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md-3-The assistant had found it, in the end, and she had found it by being wrong.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md-4-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:5:That had been weeks ago, in the year's fifth week, before the first sitting. Karis had set her a morning's work in the code's general index, which was a fat green volume with a cracked spine that nobody on the hill had read for pleasure in living memory. The task was to find every heading under which the seat's procedure for an institutional matter was set out, and to bring them back in a list, and to bring back no list at all if she was not certain. *Find it.* The assistant had been learning what Karis meant by those two words for two months, one mistake a morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md-6-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md-7-She had come up the annex stair at noon with the green volume open in both arms and a face like a girl who has broken something.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md-8-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md-9-"I think this one's wrong," she had said. "It's in the index. It's got a page. But somebody's drawn a line through it in pencil, very faint, and when I go to the page the heading's there and the text's there and nobody's used it since before the digests start." She had set the book on the table. "So I didn't put it on the list. And then I thought you'd want to know why I hadn't."
