editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:243:It was going down. It went down a little every month, and it had never stopped. Rent from waking, every day, spent or not. He had never used it, not once, not even in the last corner of the hardest fight of his life, when it had offered itself to him like a hand held out. It was sealed for a debt, and the debt was to a quiet man who sat at the middle of the bench and cut his bread into squares, and who did not know it was owed, or what for. Nobody had told him yet. The four of them had decided that at Norhold, on the night of the seal. Not yet. When it could be told whole.
