editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md-165-The house's counsel turned her steel rule four times, and each turn did something to the room before anybody in it understood what she had argued.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md-166-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md-167-At the first turn the four district evaluators stopped looking at nothing and began to look at her. At the second, the front benches put their pencils down. At the third, the hood spoke, for the only time that day. At the fourth the rule lay pointing at the platform again, as it had when she rose, and the gallery made the long low sound galleries make when they may not clap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md-168-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md-169-Karis had slid a card along the table before the counsel stood. *NAMES. PREAMBLE. SHELF. TEXT.* "She's going from the people to the paper," she had breathed. "Tick them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md-170-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:171:It was the names that had made the evaluators look up. Ilsev heard her own read out among them and wrote something small in her file without lifting her head. The counsel read twenty-two of them, one at a time, each with a grade and a house and a day, at the pace at which a person reads names in a room where names matter. The grant at Greyvane and its two renewals on paper, the season's officers under a regional registrar's seal, the tournament's, and three days of the Compact's own senior evaluator on the bluff. At the Archmarshal's name Cael thought the stillness under the hood altered, the way still water alters when something passes under it. It took four minutes. In the quiet he heard a cart's iron tyre grind over the cobbles in the street. More looking, the counsel said when she had finished, than most practitioners get in a working life; and all of it done by people who could see, sealed and minuted, across three years. She turned the rule. Tick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md-172-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md-173-It was Karis's count that made the pencils go down. Cael felt her go still beside him as the counsel gave it: seven times in the registry's whole record the integrity sections had been turned on a person, every one of the seven for something the person had done; eighty-four times on things; and not once on what a person was. Only after the count did the counsel lay her papers face down and read the room the preamble, ninety seconds of plain old words, so that the benches heard what the sections had been written for after they had heard what they had been used for. The testing of instruments. Frames, stations, records, protocols. So that the registry might know which of them it could trust.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md-174-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md-175-"Only that nobody ever has," the counsel said then, without a pause, and Cael understood from her answer what the hood had asked: whether the sections could never lawfully reach a practitioner, or only had never been made to. "That's enough. In all the years these sections have stood, this seat is asked to go first, and turn them on a person for what he is. A seat that's asked to go first deserves to hear it in plain words. The petition never used them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md-176-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md-177-Jent rose to say the petitioner had never called the use an ordinary one, and was content for the minute to say it was new. The counsel said it after him, word for word, and this once, the only time all morning, she said it over her shoulder to the benches. Third turn. Tick.
