editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md-151-So he went out into the court with his copy under his arm. The fountain was the only sound on the hill. On his way across he looked, as he always looked, at the assessment wing, and at the far end of its long front, on the ground floor, behind the counter's window, one lamp was burning. At that hour, in a wing where nobody had business before the third bell. He stood in the court and looked at it for a moment, and did not let himself guess what the man at the copying table was making. Whatever it was, it would be made properly, and it would arrive without a note.
