    83	"I asked for a witness once," he said, "with a warden across a table from me. I was right to. That man was a piece of the machine, and you don't sit down with a piece of the machine without somebody to say afterward what was said." He looked at the note. "This one's different, and I've had a year to learn how. Eleven questions in a room on the bluff. Three sentences of advisory. A chair held for him that he never asked for, two notes, and a walk out before the figure." He turned the note over. "Every move he's ever made toward me he's made in ink, and signed with his whole name. This is the first thing he's ever asked for that he can't sign. A man like that doesn't give up his ink to set a trap. He'd use the ink." He folded it. "I want to know what's worth that much to him."
    84	
    85	Nobody argued further. They did not need to; they had been heard. Karis nodded. Lira let her arms go.
    86	
    87	Seln was at the door. Nobody had heard him come up. He had the pouch under his arm and he had evidently been there for some of it, and he did not apologise for that, or come in.
    88	
    89	"He has kept every rule of a forty-year career," he said, in his counter voice. "If he means to break one for you, the breaking has been paid for somewhere you will never be shown the bill." He shifted the pouch. "Listen accordingly."
    90	
    91	Then he went down the stair to Bracken.
    92	
    93	---
    94	
    95	Downstairs, while Cael went across the court, Seln wrote his report at the document table in the front room, with the five buff folders lying in their row along the table in front of him.
    96	
    97	It was the first report of the sitting he had not had to build.
    98	
    99	He noticed that before he had written a word, and it was strange enough that he put the pen down and sat for a moment with his hands on the blotter. Every report he had sent north in a year had been built: true in every line, complete in every box the form asked for, and arranged with great care so that nothing in it led anywhere. He had built them as other men build walls, a stone at a time, checking each. This one did not need building. There was nothing to keep out of it. Eight thousand people had watched the thing. Three scribes had written it down at a speed no clerk could match. The west tower had put it in eleven words and hung them in a frame. Whatever he wrote, the world already held more of it than his form had room for.
   100	
   101	He took up the pen again.
   102	
   103	*Exhibition, finals day, against the filed Gold challenger of Auremont. Subject fought four exchanges. Capabilities exercised: as in the public record attached, entire. Result and entry: as attached. Observation in this instance is complete, the event having been public.*
   104	
   105	He read it through. Every word was true, and every word was all of the truth that was his to write. That had never happened to him before in this work. His hand wanted to go on and write the last line of the form, the line he had written at the foot of every report for a year, the one word that closed it.
   106	
   107	He held the pen over the space and did not write the word.
   108	
   109	The space stayed empty. He blotted the sheet as it was, and folded it, and sealed it, and laid it on the corner of the table for Bracken's pouch.
   110	
   111	Something in him was lighter, as a man is lighter who has set down a case he has carried so long he had forgotten it was in his hand, and he did not look at it closely. He had a long habit of not looking closely at things in himself, and he kept to it now. He sat with his hands folded on the empty blotter, as he sat at his counter in the wing at the end of a day, and let the lightness be there without asking what it was.
   112	
   113	Then, because habit is habit, he took the small book from his inside pocket and opened it and wrote in it too, a few lines, in a hand nobody at the wing had ever seen him use. He put it away.
   114	
   115	The locked case stood under the table by his chair, where it had stood every evening of the sitting, apart from the pen-case beside it. He had not opened it. He did not open it now.
   116	
   117	---
   118	
   119	The timekeepers' tower stood at the east end of the Concourse, across the outer court from the west tower where the adjudication office lived. Cael had passed under it every day of the sitting and never been inside.
   120	
   121	Brom walked with him across the court, although nobody had asked him to and he had lost the argument about the room an hour before and had not said another word about it since. They went through the people still waiting in the copy line, who turned to look, and under the arch at the tower's foot, and there Brom sat down on the step.
   122	
   123	He did it carefully, with the bad shoulder away from the stone, and put his forearms on his knees.
   124	
   125	Cael looked at him.
   126	
   127	"You said no to the room," said Brom. "So I'm not in the room. Nobody said anything about the step." He looked out across the court at the copy line. "It's a good step. I can see the clock from here."
   128	
   129	There was nothing to say to that, and Brom would not have wanted anything said. Cael went in.
   130	
   131	The stair went up round the inside of the tower, and the room was at the top of it. A timekeeper's boy was waiting at the door. He had the key already turned. He opened the door and stood aside and went off down the stair without a word, so that nobody would have to let anybody in.
   132	
   133	The room was full of clocks.
   134	
   135	They stood on shelves round all four walls, and on the long table, and two of them hung over the door. Some were brass and some were wood, and a few were in pieces, with their works laid out on cloths. Each had a card tied to it with a date and a hand. And every one of them was ticking, and no two of them said quite the same time. The room was loud with them, a dry, steady pattering. It was the timekeepers' office for the whole Concourse, where every clock the sitting used was brought to be set against the tower's great one and sent out true, and it belonged to nobody in the sitting at all.
   136	
   137	Hesk had written to him in the cold term that in a big city every clock disagrees with every other, and every one of them is certain. Cael had never been in a room that proved a letter so thoroughly.
   138	
   139	Vastin was sitting at the long table with his back to the window. He got up when Cael came in.
   140	
   141	He was in the plain grey, with nothing at the collar. He had no case with him and no papers, not a sheet, not a pencil. Cael had seen this man in a records hall, in a held chair, across a table of questions, and every time there had been a document somewhere within reach of his hand. There was nothing within reach of it now. The hands hung at his sides. The left one was not quite straight at the fingers.
   142	
   143	His eyes went over Cael once. They did it as they had on the bluff, without any hurry and without seeming to do it at all. They rested a moment where the strapping showed under the shirt, and a moment on the wrapped forearm, and on the care with which Cael lowered himself into the chair across the table. Vastin said nothing about any of it. Cael saw him take each thing and put it away, and understood that this was a kind of good manners. In his trade you did not comment on what you found. You kept it.
   144	
   145	"Thank you for coming," said Vastin, and sat.
   146	
   147	Then he frowned slightly, as a man frowns at a figure he has written down wrong.
   148	
   149	"No. Thanks are the wrong instrument. You owe me nothing, and I didn't ask you here for anything of yours. I asked you here for something of mine." He laid his hands on the table, the right flat, the left as flat as it would go. "I would rather we began straight."
   150	
   151	"Then begin," said Cael.
   152	
   153	Vastin looked round the room before he did, at the shelves of clocks.
   154	
   155	"The keeper of this room," he said, "can tell you how wrong each of these is. To the half minute. He doesn't throw them out for it. He knows each one's error, and he corrects for it, and so between them they tell him the time very well." He brought his eyes back. "That has been my work for forty years. Not finding the instrument that is right. Learning exactly how each one is wrong."
   156	
   157	He did not say what that had to do with anything. He let Cael carry it.
   158	
   159	"I closed my evaluation of you last night," he said. "On a stool by a door, at about the fourth bell. I'll not tell you what it found. You were in the ring when it was found."
   160	
   161	A clock on the wall behind him struck the half hour. Another took it up a breath later, and a third, much later, as if it had been thinking about it.
   162	
   163	"I'm going to give you eight words," said Vastin. "Four, and then four. I have watched them come across my table this year in letters about your file, the first four in the early part of it, the second four since. I want you to hear them in the order they came." He said the first four slowly, each one by itself.
   164	
   165	"*Anomaly. Irregularity. Basis. Review.*"
   166	
   167	He waited, and then said the others.
   168	
   169	"*Posture. Handling. Exposure.*" And then, after a longer space, and more quietly: "*containment.* I have seen that one twice. Both times in minutes that reached me as a kindness, with nobody expecting me to reply."
   170	
   171	Cael put the eight in a row in his head.
   172	
   173	"The first four are words you use about a fault," said Vastin. "A thing gone wrong in the record, which a careful office would like to find and mend. You find a fault so that you can take it out." He moved his left hand an inch on the wood. "The second four are words you use about a thing you mean to keep in a box. I went through the Compact's charter for them, article by article, one evening. They aren't in it. You can't write *handling* about a question. You can only write it about an answer you've already got."
   174	
   175	"Who sends you the minutes?"
   176	
   177	"I can't name him. I'm not certain there is a him. The better question took me a long while to find. Who decides which men are sent minutes, and which men are left off the list?" He said it evenly, as he would have read a line off a form. "I put it on paper once and sent it where such things are meant to go. Within the week it came back. It was very civil and there was nothing in it. I have not asked again." He looked at his left hand. "I've noticed, lately, that I have stopped meaning to. I don't care for that. I tell you so that you'll know it about me."
   178	
   179	He was quiet for a while then.
   180	
   181	The clocks went round. Down in the court somebody was calling out numbers along the copy line, and the numbers came up faintly through the window, thirty-one, thirty-two, thirty-three. Cael waited. He did not know what was coming, but he could see that it was coming, in the way the man sat, and that it had been carried a long way.
   182	
   183	Then Vastin lifted his eyes to Cael's and kept them there.
   184	
   185	"You should stop competing publicly."
   186	
   187	Cael let the clocks have a moment of it. Then he asked why.
   188	
   189	"Because the people who want this to stop are not going to use official channels much longer."
   190	
   191	---
   192	
   193	It went into the room and stayed there.
   194	
   195	Neither of them hurried it. The numbers came up from the court, forty, forty-one, and stopped. A clock on the long table ran down with a small sigh and was still, and the others went on without it. Cael sat and read the man in front of him, because that was what he had. The read said there was more, and that it would be given at its own rate. Each sentence was being paid for out of something, and the man was paying.
   196	
   197	"Who are they?"
   198	
   199	"If I gave you names I would be guessing, and I don't guess." He did not say it to be clever. He said it as Gault said a plate would not read past its last notch. "I can tell you what my year has looked like from my chair. A year ago I was asked what you are. This year I am asked what can be done about you. I answer every one of those letters by saying, again, what you are. Each answer goes up the stair. Each one is entered and filed. I am fairly sure none of them is read." He turned his left hand over, and back. "My office still works. It works very well. It has simply stopped mattering. When the questions stop being questions, an evaluator knows the decision has been taken in some room he was not shown."
   200	
   201	"There's talk of a sitting."
   202	
   203	"There will be one, after the houses have gone home. I'm called to it, and I'll go." The ghost of something went across his face. "I can't tell you what's on its paper. You won't need me to. You were there yesterday when it was written."
   204	
   205	Cael asked the next thing slowly. He knew which question it was.
   206	
   207	"The designation in my file. The old one, with no office against it. Is that part of what's coming?"
   208	
   209	Vastin did not answer.
   210	
   211	He did not look away either. He sat with his hands on the table and his eyes on Cael, and the clocks counted for him, and Cael counted with them. One. Two. Three. Four. In the whole hour it was the only silence that pressed on anything. Cael felt it the way he felt the air before a squall, heavy, all along one side.
   212	
   213	"I won't answer that one," said Vastin.
   214	
   215	That was all. But it was a great deal. He had not said *I don't know*, which would have been true and easy. He had looked for whoever set that line in the file, as Ilsev had, and found nobody; and it troubled him. It troubled him more than anything else in the hour, more than the eight words, more than the warning. He had just said a thing in this room that could end forty years if anybody heard it, and he would not say this. Cael understood that it was not the record he was wary of. It was the blank in it. A man who has spent his life learning exactly how each instrument is wrong does not like an error that belongs to no instrument at all.
   216	
   217	"If I stopped," said Cael. "If I went home and never fought where anybody could see me again. Would it change anything?"
   218	
   219	"No." Vastin did not soften it. "That is the other half of why I asked for you. What you do now makes no difference to it. A year ago it would have. A thing you do is an answer, and answers are for questions, and they have stopped asking. Nothing answers a conclusion."
   220	
   221	---
   222	
   223	The clocks went round. Cael let them, for a while. Then he set down the question he had brought up the stair with him, because he did not think the hour would have a better place for it.
   224	
   225	"How did you evaluate me?"
   226	
   227	Vastin did not ask what he meant. He looked, for a moment, very slightly pleased.
   228	
   229	"With the wrong instruments, for three days," he said. "I came up to the bluff with a file and a plate and a set of questions, and all three told me nothing. A man in my trade has one great temptation, and I gave in to it. I thought the nothing was something being hidden from me." He moved the left hand. "It is the oldest mistake there is. When an instrument won't read, you'd sooner believe it's broken than believe you've pointed it at a thing it was never made to measure."
   230	
   231	"And then?"
   232	
   233	"Then you answered my fourth question. In full, truthfully, every word of it. And I learned nothing whatever from the answer." Something that was nearly amusement moved in his face. "I have done that to other men for forty years. Nobody had ever done it to me. From that question on I stopped measuring what you could do and started watching how you went about it." He looked at Cael steadily. "The method is not a boy's. That is my whole opinion of you. It's in no file. There is no line on any form where it would go."
   234	
   235	He glanced down at his own left hand on the table, and then up again.
   236	
   237	"You're counting that," he said. "The hand."
   238	
   239	"Yes."
   240	
   241	"Good. Count it right. It's worse in the evenings now as well as the mornings. Put that down where you put things."
   242	
   243	He did not offer protection. He said, in so many words, that he had none to give, because it would have been dishonest to let the boy wonder. Whatever reach his office had, it had through the very channels he had just told Cael would be stepped round. And a warning from a man who could also shelter you, he said, would have been a smaller thing than this, because you could never be sure which of the two he was giving you.
   244	
   245	"Do you think I'll stop?" said Cael.
   246	
   247	For a moment the grey face did something Cael would spend half that night failing to name in Hesk's book.
   248	
   249	"No. I evaluated you." He let it rest a moment. "But you'll know when the channel changes, now. Knowing is worth something. It's the only thing I had to give."
   250	
   251	He stood up, and said a word of thanks to the room. He said it to the shelves and the table and the clocks, the way a man pays a small account on his way out of an inn. Then he went to the door, and out, and Cael heard his steps going down round the inside of the tower at the same even pace all the way to the bottom. He was going back down into a machine he knew to the last screw and could no longer account for.
   252	
   253	Cael stayed where he was.
   254	
   255	He gave it four minutes by the clock over the door. It may have been five by the one beside it. If he got up at once, the hour would have decided when he left, and he meant to decide that himself.
   256	
   257	Then he went round the room with his eyes before he went, as he did every room.
   258	
   259	The table bare. Not a paper, not a cup, nothing on the wood that had not been there before. The other chair, empty. Forty clocks, near enough, every one of them sure. And on the sill by the window, apart from the rest and ticking a little behind them, a small brass travelling clock not unlike Hesk's. It had a card tied to its handle in a timekeeper's hand. *Slow. Owner informed.*
   260	
   261	Nothing to file. No copy, no witness, no line in any book on the continent. The whole hour was in two heads now and nowhere else. One of those heads belonged to a man who had spent forty years making very sure nothing of the kind would ever happen to him.
   262	
   263	He went down the stair.
   264	
   265	Brom was sitting on the step under the arch with his forearms on his knees, looking at the copy line. He got up when Cael came out into the light, slowly, the bad shoulder first, and stood.
   266	
   267	"An hour," said Cael.
   268	
   269	Brom tipped his head back and looked up at the face of the tower, at the great clock every other clock in the Concourse was set by.
   270	
   271	"Fifty-one minutes."
   272	
   273	They went back across the court together. The copy line had moved perhaps twenty yards. Brom did not ask anything, all the way across, and Cael did not offer anything. At the guesting-house door Brom stopped with his hand on the latch and looked at him once, the way he looked at a floor before he stood on it.
   274	
   275	"Good," he said, and opened the door. That was the last either of them ever said about the step.

