editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-2-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-3-He closed the year that evening in Hesk's book, at the table under the window, with Hesk's steel pen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-4-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:5:It was not the end of a year by anybody's calendar but his own. But it was near enough a year since he had sat at another window, on the last night of the old term, and written down what was in the till. A thing that has been opened ought to be closed, and the ledger had been open all that time. He turned past the inventory at seventeen, and the trial, and the ring, and the minute, and the pole, and the rule, and the warning, to the next clean leaf. He ruled the margin with the edge of the closing ceremony's printed card, because he had no rule with him. Then he sat a moment before he wrote, with the pen lifted. He did that perhaps once a year and had never written it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-6-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-7-*The year, closed.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-8-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-9-*Seven now, and the one I can't account for.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-10-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-134-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-135-For the first days it was still all round them. Waystations had silver-on-grey hung at their gates, and the innkeepers came to the doors to look at the plain colours go by. Twice a rider overtook the wagons with a fresh broadside from the city, and Ephram bought both and read them out on the tailboard in what was left of his voice. The guesses had bred. There were a dozen of them now. The one word was in none.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-136-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-137-Cael read the road by its doors. Every waystation had a board beside the door, and at the first few the board still had the finals card chalked on it, every result, as if the innkeeper could not bear to rub it off. A few days on, the boards were selling hay and oats by the bushel. A few days after that a board had nothing on it but nail-holes, and the woman who kept the place asked Gault, quite kindly, what had brought two wagons so far north this early. Gault told her. She thought about it, and said she had heard there had been something on, at the coast, and wasn't that nice for them, and went to see about the soup.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-138-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:139:The old volume ran out a leaf at a time as the road did. He gave it a few lines a night, small, and most nights they said the same things. *The Wind at three on yard clay. Came when called, twice of three. Yellow creeping in at the edges of the left side. The flag stands.* Each night the things he carried had shifted a little further toward the new weight, and each night they had not finished. The healers had said a month. His own reckoning said more. The notice's warning sat at the back of his eyes where he had first read it, and did not move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-140-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-141-On the ninth day they reached the district seat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-142-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-143-The column stopped in the square outside the registry while the reserves watered the horses, and Brom got down and went in by himself. He was inside a long while. When he came out he had a folded paper in his hand. He did not look at any of them. He sat down on the wagon's tailboard beside Karis and turned it over in his fingers, the plain side up, and then he said it to the cobbles in front of his boots, all at once, before anybody could ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-144-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-166-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-167-On the twelfth day the watchers came back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-168-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-169-There were two of them, at the far end of a bridge in a market town, standing by a hired horse where the road came off the bridge into the square. Grey coats and good boots. Cael knew both faces. He had counted them for two years from the bluff's walls and windows and the ferry steps. He counted them now from the wagon's bench, and was surprised to find that he was very nearly glad of them. After a city of eight thousand who had all been looking at him at once, two men and a horse were almost homely.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-170-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:171:It went in that night next to the warning, because that was where it belonged.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-172-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:173:*Two of them, at a bridge. The old arrangement, two and a horse. After Norhold it looks small, and I find I'm fond of it. But Vastin spent an hour telling me the old way is finishing. So: noted, and kept beside his hour. They may be the last watchers I get whose names I could learn.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-174-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-175-On the fourteenth day Karis closed the year's papers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-176-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-177-She did it at a waystation table after supper, and the whole ceremony of it was that she would not have one. She took out everything the year had made, from the cases it had ridden in: the clause and the four pages, the qualifying letters, the circular, the rulings, the trial rules, the nine charts on the backs of old seating plans, the entry. She sorted it all into two piles on the board. She tied each with tape and wrote a contents leaf for each and put the leaf on top, and the bundles went back into the case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-178-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-1-# Chapter 59 — Same Build
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-2-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:3:Vastin went down the tower stair at his ordinary pace and out under the arch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-4-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:5:There was a big young man sitting on the step there with bandaged forearms, watching the court. He looked up as Vastin passed. Vastin knew him from the row: the continental Copper champion, the bluff's wall. He nodded to him, as one nods to a man keeping a door. The young man nodded back, gravely, and went on watching the court.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-6-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-7-He crossed it slowly. The copy line still ran along the colonnade, and he walked down the length of it and nobody in it knew him. A woman with a basket of eels. Three boys from some hill house arguing about the fourth exchange with their hands. A grey delegation head in Auremont's blue, standing in his place with his hands folded behind him, who did know him, and inclined his head an inch, and was answered by an inch. At the end of the colonnade, by the north tunnel, there was a frame on a nail with a sheet in it. He stopped in front of it and read the eleven words again, although he had heard them read aloud at dawn and could have said them in his sleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-8-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-9-He had written a note like that himself, once, on the road home from the bluff. Fewer words, and in a worse light, and signed with his whole name. He had thought it careful at the time. He saw now that the west tower had done it better. They had not tried to say what the boy was. They had said only what their instrument could and could not do, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-10-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-46-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-47-*I keep waiting for that sentence to feel like a wound. It feels like the truth arriving.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-48-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-49----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-50-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:51:The long table had all of them that night. Gault, and Rooke, and Bracken at the end with the pouch, and Withrow at the head with her cup. The two reserves were wide-eyed at the bottom of it. Seln sat at the far corner, on the margin, where he had sat all sitting. Cael told them the hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-52-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:53:He gave it to them in two piles, as Karis had taught him to give anything that mattered. First what had been said, word for word, as near as he could get it. Then, separately, and saying so, what he had made of it. The telling ran longer than the hour. Nobody interrupted. Karis wrote it all down without looking up once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-54-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-55-When he had finished, Lira was first, because somebody had to argue against and she could do it better than anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-56-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-57-"Or," she said, "it's the cleverest thing they've done all year." She put her elbows on the table. "They can't order you off a floor. There's no rule for it. Karis would tear it to pieces in a week. So they send up the one honest man they've got, and he sits you down in a room full of clocks and asks you very kindly to walk off the floor yourself. That's what they want. They can't make you. He can ask." She looked round the table. "Honest men get sent to do that sort of thing. That's what honest men are for, to people like that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-58-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-64-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-65-"Grade the man." He turned his cup round in his bandaged hands. "He came all that way himself, by the post road, with one bag. He sat in that row the whole afternoon. Then he sat all night on a stool by a door in a room with nothing in it for him, and wrote nothing down. You don't spend a night like that on a thing you're only managing." He put the cup down. "That's all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-66-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-67-Karis lifted her pen off the page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-68-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:69:"Then grade it both ways," she said, "and keep both. As a report it's poor. As a man he's good. One doesn't cancel the other. What it means is that the warning is true and we can't prove it, both at once." She looked at the page. "That's the worst kind of warning there is. It's also the only kind anybody ever gets in time to use."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-70-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-71-There was a little quiet. Then Seln spoke from his corner, in his counter voice, without looking up from the pouch he had open in his lap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-72-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:73:"Wardens threaten. Politicians hint. That was neither." The pouch's strap went through its buckle under his fingers. "That was an honest man spending career capital to tell you the truth a few months early. Treat the warning as real, because it is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-74-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-75-Nobody argued with that. At that table, nobody arguing was not the same as agreeing because they had to. It meant there was nothing left to say.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-76-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-77-Lira let out a breath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-78-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-110-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-111-They went up one at a time, Karis with her papers and Brom with his cup, and Lira last, and Lira stopped on the stair, and he thought for a moment that she would say something, and she did not. She only knocked twice on the doorframe as she passed, as she had knocked on his head on the night of the party, and went on up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-112-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-113-He sat on alone with the window open, and wrote the night's last entry, slowly, while the city put itself to bed below.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-114-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:115:*First honest warning I've ever gotten from inside the machine. He built no case, made no ask, took no note. He just told me the weather. The Compact's best evaluator thinks the next thing that comes for me won't wear a uniform or file a challenge. Write it down. Believe it. Keep competing anyway — but from tonight, we build like people who believe it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-116-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-117----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-118-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-119-The closing began at the second bell of the morning, and the Concourse filled for it one last time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-120-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-60-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-61-She went. They heard her go all the way down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-62-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-63-Cael unfolded it. It was a half sheet in a spare upright hand he did not know, though he could have guessed whose it was. There was no seal and no heading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-64-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:65:*The Archmarshal Vastin asks the enrollee for one hour, before the houses leave the city. Nothing will be written by either party. It would be better if nothing were brought to write with. The timekeepers' office in the east tower has lent a room for the purpose; it belongs to neither the adjudication office nor the Compact. He is free to say no; it will count against him nowhere, with no one, at any time; and that sentence is not a formality.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-66-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-67-He read it aloud, because they would have asked him to. Then he laid it on the rocking table, and they all looked at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-68-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-69-Karis spoke first. She always did when a paper was on the table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-70-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-134-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-135-They stood on shelves round all four walls, and on the long table, and two of them hung over the door. Some were brass and some were wood, and a few were in pieces, with their works laid out on cloths. Each had a card tied to it with a date and a hand. And every one of them was ticking, and no two of them said quite the same time. The room was loud with them, a dry, steady pattering. It was the timekeepers' office for the whole Concourse, where every clock the sitting used was brought to be set against the tower's great one and sent out true, and it belonged to nobody in the sitting at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-136-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-137-Hesk had written to him in the cold term that in a big city every clock disagrees with every other, and every one of them is certain. Cael had never been in a room that proved a letter so thoroughly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-138-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:139:Vastin was sitting at the long table with his back to the window. He got up when Cael came in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-140-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-141-He was in the plain grey, with nothing at the collar. He had no case with him and no papers, not a sheet, not a pencil. Cael had seen this man in a records hall, in a held chair, across a table of questions, and every time there had been a document somewhere within reach of his hand. There was nothing within reach of it now. The hands hung at his sides. The left one was not quite straight at the fingers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-142-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:143:His eyes went over Cael once. They did it as they had on the bluff, without any hurry and without seeming to do it at all. They rested a moment where the strapping showed under the shirt, and a moment on the wrapped forearm, and on the care with which Cael lowered himself into the chair across the table. Vastin said nothing about any of it. Cael saw him take each thing and put it away, and understood that this was a kind of good manners. In his trade you did not comment on what you found. You kept it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-144-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:145:"Thank you for coming," said Vastin, and sat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-146-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-147-Then he frowned slightly, as a man frowns at a figure he has written down wrong.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-148-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-149-"No. Thanks are the wrong instrument. You owe me nothing, and I didn't ask you here for anything of yours. I asked you here for something of mine." He laid his hands on the table, the right flat, the left as flat as it would go. "I would rather we began straight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-150-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-151-"Then begin," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-152-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:153:Vastin looked round the room before he did, at the shelves of clocks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-154-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-155-"The keeper of this room," he said, "can tell you how wrong each of these is. To the half minute. He doesn't throw them out for it. He knows each one's error, and he corrects for it, and so between them they tell him the time very well." He brought his eyes back. "That has been my work for forty years. Not finding the instrument that is right. Learning exactly how each one is wrong."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-156-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-157-He did not say what that had to do with anything. He let Cael carry it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-158-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-159-"I closed my evaluation of you last night," he said. "On a stool by a door, at about the fourth bell. I'll not tell you what it found. You were in the ring when it was found."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-160-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-161-A clock on the wall behind him struck the half hour. Another took it up a breath later, and a third, much later, as if it had been thinking about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-162-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:163:"I'm going to give you eight words," said Vastin. "Four, and then four. I have watched them come across my table this year in letters about your file, the first four in the early part of it, the second four since. I want you to hear them in the order they came." He said the first four slowly, each one by itself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-164-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-165-"*Anomaly. Irregularity. Basis. Review.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-166-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-167-He waited, and then said the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-168-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-169-"*Posture. Handling. Exposure.*" And then, after a longer space, and more quietly: "*containment.* I have seen that one twice. Both times in minutes that reached me as a kindness, with nobody expecting me to reply."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-170-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-171-Cael put the eight in a row in his head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-172-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:173:"The first four are words you use about a fault," said Vastin. "A thing gone wrong in the record, which a careful office would like to find and mend. You find a fault so that you can take it out." He moved his left hand an inch on the wood. "The second four are words you use about a thing you mean to keep in a box. I went through the Compact's charter for them, article by article, one evening. They aren't in it. You can't write *handling* about a question. You can only write it about an answer you've already got."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-174-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-175-"Who sends you the minutes?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-176-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-177-"I can't name him. I'm not certain there is a him. The better question took me a long while to find. Who decides which men are sent minutes, and which men are left off the list?" He said it evenly, as he would have read a line off a form. "I put it on paper once and sent it where such things are meant to go. Within the week it came back. It was very civil and there was nothing in it. I have not asked again." He looked at his left hand. "I've noticed, lately, that I have stopped meaning to. I don't care for that. I tell you so that you'll know it about me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-178-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-179-He was quiet for a while then.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-180-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-181-The clocks went round. Down in the court somebody was calling out numbers along the copy line, and the numbers came up faintly through the window, thirty-one, thirty-two, thirty-three. Cael waited. He did not know what was coming, but he could see that it was coming, in the way the man sat, and that it had been carried a long way.
