editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-79-He had watched them build barges on the slip below Ostrand bridge. He had stood on the bank on the house's free afternoons for whole hours, because the work had an order in it and he liked orders. The shipwrights did not start with the sides you could see. They laid one long timber down the middle of the slip first, end to end, and squared it, and pinned it, and nothing else went on until it was true. Then the ribs went up from it on both sides, and everything that came after hung from the ribs. A barge could lose a plank and float. If the keel was wrong, nothing you nailed to it afterward would keep the river out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md-80-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:81:"Everything else hangs from this," said Karis. "Every argument in the answer comes back to one fact: a seat has already heard this question and settled it on the public record, and the paper we're answering doesn't say otherwise. It doesn't even try. So the first thing on the table is the place where the question was settled." She put her palm on the transcript and left it there. "And then I don't move it."
