editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-editorial-prompt.md-1370-"Walking them for what?" said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-editorial-prompt.md-1371-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-editorial-prompt.md:1372:"So that the gate can shut on every field the same morning." Seln laid one fingertip beside each bean in turn as he spoke, not touching any of them. "Chapter fourteen lets the registry restrict an unclassified person: his standing, his papers, his endorsements, everything that's tied to him. A restriction that runs office by office leaks. Somebody hears, somebody moves a deed, somebody's cousin carries a letter across a district line before the freeze arrives. So before anything runs, a clerk goes round every fence the man has. Who sponsors him. Which houses hold his papers. What his people own, and where they own it. The tin box under the bench." His voice did not change at all on that. "Every gate is found and counted first. Then on the morning it's wanted, every gate shuts at once."
