editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-002/review-editorial.md-219-- **The second and third sittings come quickly.** The spine needs the preliminary ruling (item 8, M3) before Lira's first meet (item 10, M4). M2 dates the meet three times: "six weeks away" on about day 26 (ch11:99), "Five weeks from now" on day 30 (ch12:173), and "four weeks away now" on day 42 (ch13:247). That puts it at day 65–70. So the second and third sittings fall on days 47 and 54 (or 61), and Bracken's "eleven, expect fourteen" is front-loaded. That is fine, but say it once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-002/review-editorial.md-220-- **The nine-week inventory clock.** The plan's "pre-restriction inventory (nine weeks; they are in the sixth)" (item 7) must be counted from service on day 8, so that the sixth week is days 43–49. Counted from the year's first day, the sixth week ended with this movement.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-002/review-editorial.md-221-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-002/review-editorial.md:222:**Brom's ninety days.** The summons was collected on Book 5's road day 9 (B5 ch60:145, "before ninety days are out"). M1 dates the entry "about two months after the seventeen-day road", so the day-4 docket falls about 72 days in, "with weeks in hand" (M1 ch3:41). The ninety therefore close about day 22. Ch12's confirmation on day 31 reads "*The panel is constituted*" (ch12:225). That is the source's wording ("panel constituted"), but after M1's "This convenes them, on paper" it can be heard as a constitution outside the window. Change one phrase: "*The panel convened under this office's docket of the fourth day is constituted as follows:*", or "is confirmed". This is not a must-fix, but the coordinator asked about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-002/review-editorial.md-223-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-002/review-editorial.md-224-**2. The lane's slope (ten, ten, twelve).** Accept the slope as canon. It is consistent with M1: the first two lanes cost the same, the bill grows only with close spending, and it is untested past three. Lira stopping it is in character and true to Rooke. The slope is accepted on condition that the number is measured right (§4.2 item 3). Record it in the ledger as "slow through twelve, clean at thirteen, on the third lane in one night".
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-002/review-editorial.md-225-
--
