editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-002/recheck-r2.stdout.log-1712-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-002/recheck-r2.stdout.log-1713-Brom's paperwork arrived in two envelopes in the same week, and he opened them in different rooms.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-002/recheck-r2.stdout.log-1714-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-002/recheck-r2.stdout.log:1715:The first was the district seat's: the advancement review's formal docket confirmation, panel constituted, the last Fifth-day of term, the season record entered as evidence, a line at the foot Bracken read aloud at the counter with the satisfaction of a registrar adding one to a tally he had kept for thirty years — *outcome anticipated: advancement, per title.* "They've written *anticipated* on a docket," Bracken said. "The registry does not write *anticipated* on dockets. Somebody at the district seat has decided the ink is cheaper than the pretense." He handed it over. "Iron-tier, by the last Fifth-day. The plaque's ribbon can go on your collar now. Nobody will think it's presumption."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-002/recheck-r2.stdout.log-1716-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-002/recheck-r2.stdout.log-1717-Brom read it once and folded it and put it in the kit, and the ribbon stayed in the kit, and the table noted where the ribbon stayed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-002/recheck-r2.stdout.log-1718-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-002/recheck-r2.stdout.log-1886-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-002/recheck-r2.stdout.log-1887-"It isn't in dispute." Jent said it without malice, without emphasis, without anything at all — a man stating the weather. "The documentation is conceded. The provision is conceded. The petition doesn't require either to be defective, and I'd be wasting the seat's time and yours to pretend otherwise." He squared the exhibit list. "The petition turns on the integrity question and the re-evaluation remedy. Those are matters of the registry's discretion. I'll argue them on the schedule, and you'll answer, and the seat will rule." He stood, at the correct height. "I look forward to the sessions. Chancellor. Counsel. Advocate." A pause, and the only sentence he addressed to the enrollee: "Sir." And he was gone, at a clerk's unhurried pace, leaving the conference exactly as long as the code required and not a minute longer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-002/recheck-r2.stdout.log-1888-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-002/recheck-r2.stdout.log:1889:The counsel's own read came in the coach on the way back, unprompted, delivered to the window in the voice of an advocate revising twenty-six years of assumptions in real time. "He's not there to argue. I've faced men sent to argue — they have a tell, a place they lean, a clause they love. He has none. He's there to *conduct* — to move the petition through the schedule at the code's pace and let the seat do what the seat was constituted to do." She turned from the window. "I have never lost a case to a man who didn't want to win it. I'm not certain I've ever faced one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-002/recheck-r2.stdout.log-1890-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-002/recheck-r2.stdout.log-1891-Seln, from the coach's end, without opening his eyes: "You've faced hundreds. They're called clerks. You've just never been on the other side of the counter."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-002/recheck-r2.stdout.log-1892-
