editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log-3227--Somebody at Ostrand had learned it very well indeed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log-3228-+He knew the shape of it now from the other side. He had taught it to his juniors as the decent way to say no: it claimed no weighing it had not done, it argued nothing, it left the man outside with nothing in his hands to be angry at. Somebody at Ostrand had learned it very well. He thought, for no reason he could give, of a field assessor he had once refused in that form, years ago, who had left the service two years after and gone to a guild on the coast. He had never found out whether the man's extra page would have been right. That had been the whole convenience of the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log-3229- 
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:3230:-He went to the first sitting's minute, which was still in the grey-taped bundle, and laid it beside the denial. Five days ago, at the wool exchange, the five chairs of the evaluation seat had sat under grey covers, and the minute said *to be called*. This morning the same seat was *fully constituted*. Somewhere in the four days between that sitting and this refusal, then, somebody had filled five chairs. He knew every senior evaluator in that district by name and grade. Not one of them had written to him for the boy's file, which any of them would have needed before they sat; and none of the five chairs had been offered to the one officer who had already sat across a table from the boy with an instrument.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log-3231-+He struck the half-line through, corner to corner, so that it could still be read. On a clean sheet he wrote the refusal again: the section the hall had failed to meet, the officer it might apply to instead, the date by which it should. It took him a quarter of an hour. He signed it with his name entire and put it in *done*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log-3232- 
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log-3233--That told him more than the sentences did. A schedule drawn to find something out would have wanted him in it. This one had been drawn by somebody who already knew what it would find, and had left no chair for anyone who might find something else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log-3234-+Then he took a slip of paper and wrote a question on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log-3235- 
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log-3236--And it cited nothing. His petition had stood on two provisions, the old and the new. The answer stood on none, and he understood that this was not carelessness but the point: a ruling has legs, and can be walked up to a higher office and knocked over there. This had no legs. It was a shut door with nobody's name on it, and it had cost whoever shut it a sheet of good paper and the time it takes to sand two lines.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:3237:+*Minute of the 40th: evaluation seat, to be called. Refusal of the 44th: fully constituted. Who sat down in those five chairs in four days, without asking this office for the file?*
