editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log-6367-books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-19.md:59:Cael laid the arithmetic on the table because the arithmetic had already been laid on all of them by a season: surviving Gold required everything. The Shield captain had priced Silver at a handspan twice — with the suite constrained to documented rates. Daeva's lane, at trial traffic, had cost the full public repertoire spent openly just to vacate ground. A filed bout at her full intent, against the banking doctrine's public-suite-only discipline, was not a fight; it was a stoppage with preliminaries. The doctrine that had governed every public act of his life since Vell's circuit — show only what the record holds; bank the rest — had reached the fight it could not survive.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log-6368-books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md:98:*Adversarial, earnest — non-hostile.* Every entry in the log was adversarial; the stakes rule demanded it and had demanded it since a boy in a market town first understood what the architecture would and would not accept. But the engagement field had never carried *non-hostile* in eight notices — not from Reydan, who had fought him to end him and then wanted the answer afterward; not from Daeva, who had fought him with a Gold-tier's whole seriousness and no malice at all and had still, on the day, been trying to take him apart. This was a machine's grammar bending to describe a fight that was entirely in earnest and entirely without enmity, which was as close as the architecture had ever come to writing down the word friendship, and could not, and had reached for the nearest thing in its vocabulary and found a negative.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log-6369-editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-14.md:65:Four times before this, in forty years, he had sent a finding to a proceeding that had not called him. A district that had challenged a house's enrollment, a dozen years back: his finding had gone in against the district's own petition, and the seat had weighed it and the district had lost, and the district had written to him afterward in perfectly good temper to say so. A hill station's frame that read high: the frame had come out of its housing on his evidence. A guild hall. A sponsorship quarrel so old he could barely recall the guild. Four times offered. Four times taken in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:6370:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-14.md:151:He went to the first sitting's minute, which was still in the grey-taped bundle, and laid it beside the denial. Five days ago, at the wool exchange, the five chairs of the evaluation seat had sat under grey covers, and the minute said *to be called*. This morning the same seat was *fully constituted*. Somewhere in the four days between that sitting and this refusal, then, somebody had filled five chairs. He knew every senior evaluator in that district by name and grade. Not one of them had written to him for the boy's file, which any of them would have needed before they sat; and none of the five chairs had been offered to the one officer who had already sat across a table from the boy with an instrument.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log-6371-editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-14.md:163:Then he struck it through, corner to corner, so that it could still be read underneath, and on a clean sheet he wrote the refusal again, longer, giving the station the section it had failed to meet and the one officer it might apply to instead. It took him a quarter of an hour where the old line would have taken a quarter of a minute. He signed it with his name entire and put it in *done*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log-6372-books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-19.md:29:She had lost the crown nine days ago in a semifinal that had taken four exchanges and one very long minute, and had lost it to the woman about to fight for it, and the brass mark had stayed beside her name on the standings sheet all that time because the mark did not move until the final moved it. In about forty minutes a circlet of hammered copper that had been on her shelf for two years was going to be handed to somebody else in front of eleven hundred people.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log-6373-books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-19.md:49:Lira's leading hip was nine days old. She had not favoured it in the covered walk that morning and she was not favouring it now, which meant nothing, because Lira could conceal a limp the way other people concealed a yawn; what could not be concealed was the way she set her feet at the mark. She set the leading one first and let the weight arrive onto it in two stages instead of one. Two stages. He had watched her set her feet at a mark ninety times this season and it had been one stage every time.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log-6803-editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:618:- **Vastin and his clerk:** the stopped pen; the candle; "Later than usual. But yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log-6804-editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:622:- **Daeva's statement** filed at the seat (day 46), received; read into the minute at the third sitting under *statements entered* and set aside.
