editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log-9808-editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:185:*Pending a proceeding worth sending it to.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log-9809-editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:79:*Two polite men from the registry came through Denvash asking after my trade licenses and the shop's title. Wanted to know if you had any interest recorded in either. I told them my grandson's interest in this shop is that he grew up in it, and they wrote that down. Whatever they're building, it has a shape. Mind the shape. — H.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log-9810-editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:131:"For a smith with a forge and a wife and a guild, six weeks. Ten, if the wife's people are spread about." He looked at the four in their row and the one apart. "For a boy with friends at three provincial registries, and a house that took him in, and a grandfather with a shop at the far end of the country—" He laid one finger flat on the board beside the fifth bean. "Nine weeks. They are in the sixth."
