editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:10694:   151	He went to the first sitting's minute, which was still in the grey-taped bundle, and laid it beside the denial. Five days ago, at the wool exchange, the five chairs of the evaluation seat had sat under grey covers, and the minute said *to be called*. This morning the same seat was *fully constituted*. Somewhere in the four days between that sitting and this refusal, then, somebody had filled five chairs. He knew every senior evaluator in that district by name and grade. Not one of them had written to him for the boy's file, which any of them would have needed before they sat; and none of the five chairs had been offered to the one officer who had already sat across a table from the boy with an instrument.
