79:Cael did not need to be told what was under the hand. A square cream envelope with a dark red seal on it, unbroken, which had come up the hill twenty-three days ago and had not been opened since. Under it, the summons, the docket, the district's confirmation with *outcome anticipated* on it in a clerk's round hand, and a sitting on the last Fifth-day of the year at which three assessors and a sigil frame would almost certainly find Brom an Iron. His family's ledger had been waiting for that line since before he could lift a practice weight. In the second box was a grandmother at the head of a long table, and a word he had never once said in the same breath as *east*.
113:The return was on the copying table where he had left it at the sixth bell, sealed and addressed, ready for the morning bag.
129:The boy's window, on the residence passage, had a lamp in it too. The boy would be at his sill with the old instrument-maker's book, writing his one line. Seln had never asked to read a word of it, and never would. It was the one record on the hill he had decided, a long time ago and without telling anybody, not to know.
137:It was a small brass key worn bright at the bow, and it had lived in that pocket for longer than he had lived in any room. He held it on his palm in the pale square of light. Above him, on the shelf over the copying table, in the shadow, was the thing it opened. He did not look up at the shelf. He looked at the key.
161:Somewhere in what he kept were copies twelve years old, of a long dull run of ledgers labelled *maintenance records*. He had made them on his fourth posting, in a room he had every right to be in, because he made copies of things; he had made copies of his mother's ledgers as a boy, for the pleasure of seeing two hands come out the same. A clerk in a green corridor, a year after he made them, had come out of a side door and asked whether he was unwell, because he had stopped dead in the middle of the floor between two ordinary appointments. He had said no. He had kept the appointment. He had not been unwell. He had been reading, in his head, all at once, what the copies said.
63:Brom had been telling Lira about strapping. Nobody east of anywhere cut it the way he liked; he would take all of it. And in the middle of the strapping his right hand had come off the table and gone to the left side of his coat, to the inside pocket, and lain flat over the cloth, and stayed there while he went on talking, as if it had gone there without him.
79:Cael did not need to be told what was under the hand. A square cream envelope with a dark red seal on it, unbroken, which had come up the hill twenty-three days ago and had not been opened since. Under it, the summons, the docket, the district's confirmation with *outcome anticipated* on it in a clerk's round hand, and a sitting on the last Fifth-day of the year at which three assessors and a sigil frame would almost certainly find Brom an Iron. His family's ledger had been waiting for that line since before he could lift a practice weight. In the second box was a grandmother at the head of a long table, and a word he had never once said in the same breath as *east*.
165:The return still lay on the table, square to its edge, and he looked at the printed headings down its left side, which he had filled in so many times that he no longer saw them. *Subject. Attendance. Examination. Figure. Associations. Conduct. Remarks.* Every heading on the form was a question about the subject. There was not one line anywhere on it, and never had been on any form of the service's that he had ever filled in, for a question about the rule the subject was being measured by, or whose rule it was, or since when. He had paper that showed whose question it had been all along. As far as he knew, nobody else alive had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:103:They were from the dairy. He had written them on the way down in the coach that morning, out of habit, to have the argument by him in case anybody in that room should ever ask him for it: Denvash and the brass plate; Vell's ledger; the Greyvane hearing; the grant and two renewals on paper; the inspection; the clause at Norhold; Umber's *no number goes here*. His receipts. He had laid them out a week ago, in order, at full strength, and Karis had called them an induction and he had not had an answer for her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:153:Cael saw it from the covered walk, a little after the second bell on the Sixth-day morning, on his way across to the wing. It was a small thing. In two years he had never once seen that window shut in daylight. The lodge-man sat behind it on his high stool from the first bell to the last, with his tea and the bluff road in front of him, and talked to anybody who came up it. Now the shutter came across the glass from the inside, and a moment later the lodge door opened, and the lodge-man stood in it without his hat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:79:Cael did not need to be told what was under the hand. A square cream envelope with a dark red seal on it, unbroken, which had come up the hill twenty-three days ago and had not been opened since. Under it, the summons, the docket, the district's confirmation with *outcome anticipated* on it in a clerk's round hand, and a sitting on the last Fifth-day of the year at which three assessors and a sigil frame would almost certainly find Brom an Iron. His family's ledger had been waiting for that line since before he could lift a practice weight. In the second box was a grandmother at the head of a long table, and a word he had never once said in the same breath as *east*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:83:The cups went round. Lira took hers in both hands and put her boots up on the bench end, and dug her thumbs into the side of her landing hip, which was Rooke's nightly order and which she obeyed with the face of somebody paying a fine. In three days she would be on a strange floor two days north. She blew across her tea before anybody else had spoken.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-23.md:3:The colors came out of their trunk on the Sixth-day morning, before the sun was over the wall, and smelled of cedar and the inside of a cupboard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:17:On a floor, if you went to the same place three times, the other fighter learned where you liked to go. A carter was no different. Ask one man three questions about the east road and he would remember you in a week, and tell the next man who stood him a cup, and the next man might wear a grey coat. So she had split her question in three, and gone to three people on three days, and asked each of them about one thing that had nothing to do with the other two. Afterward she could add them up herself, on the walk back up the hill, where nobody could see her do the sum.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:19:The first had been an old man with a mule team, three days before. Rooke really had wanted the new frame cord, and the river barge really had brought it down, and she really had been the only one in the program with a free hour on a Second-day morning. She had carried his manifest for him from the barge to his cart while he complained about the mules, and she had asked him, idly, what all the stamps on it were for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:63:He was young, not much older than Brom, with a new wagon and a quiet way of standing beside it, and she found him on the First-day in the slack hour before noon, when the ferryman ate his bread and the line did not move. She had come down with a parcel of the Stone's letters for the river post and a list of tape for the chandler, and both errands were true, and she did them first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:95:Up there, pinned to the east hall door in Rooke's square capitals, was a card with her name on it for the program's first road meet. Rooke had crossed out its date a week ago and chalked another eight days later, when the confluence's stewards wrote that the meet was put back for repairs to their hall; she had minded for an afternoon and then been glad of the eight days. Up there was a red drill-book with a figure in it that was hers, and a broad patient man from the river academy who had written to ask whether he might come and teach her, after she beat him. Up there was a word she had never had before this year, and had to learn to say: *program*. Somebody had built a ladder, and leaned it against a wall, and put her name at the foot of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:139:"Fifth-day," she said, before he had asked anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:167:"Fifth-day," she said again, and went in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:171:Cael was first down the dairy step on the Fifth-day, and Lira was already there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:177:He read it twice and did not ask. She had said Fifth-day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:205:"I'm not asking about the filings. I know what Karis does is the best paper this house has ever sent down the hill. I know we win, every sitting." She put the chalk down on the table. "And I know that a fortnight ago a hood read *lawful* and *proceeding* into the minute in one breath, and went on to the next item. Every time we win, it goes on. So I'm putting it on the table. Once. Properly, with everybody here. Then it's been asked, and nobody can say afterward that it wasn't."

